{"title":"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Mike and Alain play a game in which each player is equally likely to win. The first player to win three games becomes the champion, and no further games are played. If Mike has won the first game, what is the probability that Mike becomes the champion?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b555ee3d0491fd447f3ee71ef1791b17f9df8fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{5}{8} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{11}{16} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"380\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If Mike won the first game, then he needs to win two more games. Let the letter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> represent Mike winning a game and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> represent Alain winning a game. The possible cases are then:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/7/5c762fede331ca4664d091abfd2b1efc3332fe88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MMM, MAMM, MMAM, MAMAM, MAAMM, MMAAM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"499\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Since they're equally likely to win, each sequence of games with the same length has the same probability. A sequence of length three has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c532817f371361b757a1afade638f8606ae21c38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{1}{2})^{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"38\" /> probability but there is only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, a sequence of length four has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b76a0cdaf0402eb3b8e6bc72f8cb9bfa03c7b583.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{1}{2})^{3} = \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"38\" /> probability but there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and a sequence of length five has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f2add953526291717bedfc83f1284d7278523b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{1}{2})^{4} = \\frac{1}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" /> probability but there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This means that the total probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/1/451d13103b5d4be76ddd8a52754a3c7e13df1e9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{3}{16} = \\frac{4}{16} + \\frac{4}{16} + \\frac{3}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/8216dff816c70317def5600d520de34dc33a87e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(C) } \\frac{11}{16}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 24 (page does not exist)\">Problem 24</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2017 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_C